  "notes": {
    "forAnalyst": "Returns and refunds after the sale: what came back, why, and what the customer gets.",
    "forArchitect": "Three entities (the return, its lines and its attachments) and eight dependencies: no longer the light add-on it was, but a full part of the order lifecycle. Thirteen settings carry the policy — which order and shipment statuses allow a return, the return window, reasons, attachments and notifications — so a change of policy is not a release.",
    "forDeveloper": "It raises three events (`ReturnChangingEvent`, `ReturnChangedEvent`, `ReturnStatusChangedEvent`) for downstream systems; the money movement itself belongs to the order's refund document. Model the physical return and the financial refund as separate steps, because they fail independently.",
    "owns": [
      "ReturnEntity"
    ],
    "reachForItWhen": [
      "Customers send goods back and expect money or credit",
      "Return reasons and windows are business policy that changes",
      "Warehouse and finance need separate signals"
    ],
    "doNotReachForItWhen": [
      "You only need to cancel an unshipped order — that is an order status change",
      "The refund is the whole requirement: Orders owns the payment document",
      "Exchange logistics: that is a new order plus a return, not one document"
    ]
  },
